// A networked game where every player is flying something.
//
// Box3D does the simulation, ashiato owns the rewind, ashiato-sync owns the wire -- the
// same three pieces as src/driving, arranged around one premise: A PILOT HAS NO WORLD
// POSE. See cockpit_components.hpp, which argues it. Everything here follows from it, and
// what follows is mostly things that are ABSENT:
//
//   - No character capsule, no hand colliders, no contact filtering. A pilot is three
//     poses inside a seat; nothing about them touches physics, so nothing about them can
//     push, be pushed, or need a filter written to stop it.
//   - No composition of a pilot into world space, anywhere, on either side of the wire.
//   - No fractional-tick display sampling. The renderer is handed the state at each tick
//     and interpolates between the last two itself, which is one mechanism it can see and
//     verify rather than two clocks that have to agree.
//
// What IS here for smoothness, because the requirement is that the cockpit never shakes:
//
//   - vehicle_states() reports a DISPLAY pose, computed once per tick, which is the
//     simulation's pose plus a visual offset that absorbs rollback corrections and decays.
//     A correction moves the simulation immediately and the picture gradually.
//   - The tick rate is a setting, so the bandwidth trade can be made without touching a
//     compile.

